# DC Safety Constraints

## Adversarial Audits are READ-ONLY

When asked to "audit", "destroy test", "break this", or similar:
- NEVER delete files, remove functionality, or replace working code
- NEVER modify source files during an audit
- Report findings as text; only fix when explicitly asked in a SEPARATE step

## Ratchet Principle

- NEVER disable, weaken, or remove a TypeScript strict flag or lint rule
- If a rule causes friction, fix the code -- do not relax the rule
- Targeted inline disable comments with an explanation are acceptable

## No Type Assertions (`as`)

NEVER use `as` to cast types. Use type guards, generic return types, or fix the type definition.
Allowed: `as const` (narrowing), third-party library boundaries with a comment.

## No String-Based Type Discrimination

NEVER use `.includes()`, `.toLowerCase()`, or regex to determine types, statuses, or routing.
Use typed const objects validated at the boundary.

## No Destructive Git Operations

NEVER run `git push --force`, `git reset --hard`, `DROP TABLE`, `DROP DATABASE`, or recursive `rm -rf` on system paths.

## No Hardcoded Person IDs

NEVER hardcode individual person IDs (Slack user IDs, GitHub user IDs, email addresses). Resolve dynamically at runtime.

## Code Quality

- Fix all lint/type errors before completing work
- Delete dead code immediately; no commented-out code
- No `_unusedVar` prefixes -- remove the variable entirely
